  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Thursday)\nIssued at 133 PM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nCooler weather is settling into the region today. It's being\ndriven into the area by strong northerly to northwesterly winds.\nHighest reported wind gusts (as of 1PM) have been upwards of 40 \nto 50 mph. For tonight, winds will slowly diminish through this\nevening but speeds will really decrease mainly after 9PM.\nIsolated rain and thundershowers are possible in the area that \nstretches from NE WY to NW SD later this afternoon through \ntonight. Severe weather is not expected and rain amounts won't add\nup to much.\n\nLooking ahead to the weekend, temperatures will be at or below\nnormal for this time of year with highs reaching the upper 60s to\n70s. Winds will slowly swing back to the east and southeast on\nSaturday with wind speeds really increasing Sunday, afternoon\ngusts will be upwards of 30 to 45 mph, strongest speeds in the SD\nplains.\n\nIsolated rain and thundershower chances return to the SD plains\nSaturday into Saturday night but amounts will be on the lighter\nside, mainly less than a quarter inch if anything. But Sunday into\nMonday, a passing upper level trough north of the CWA will track \neastward. At the surface, low pressure will develop Sunday\nafternoon and likely travel northeast. As it does, thunderstorms\nare likely to track through the area late Sunday into Sunday\nnight. Some of those storms could be strong to severe. The SPC and\nmachine learning severe weather models are highlighting a marginal\nrisk of severe weather. Rainfall amounts Sunday into early Monday\nwill be mainly less than a half inch with locally higher amounts\npossible.\n\nNext week is looking dry with low-end chances for precip late in\nthe week with highs near or below normal for this time of year.",
